var tafsirData_46 = {
  "1": "The surah opens with the two Arabic letters Ḥâ and Mĩm, known among the disconnected letters that appear at the beginning of twenty-nine chapters of the Quran. Their precise meaning is a matter of the unseen, and the soundest position of the early scholars is that Allah alone knows their full reality. Yet their placement is far from meaningless. These letters immediately seize the attention of the listener, especially an Arab of the Prophet’s time who recognised that the very letters of his own alphabet were being used to compose a Book unlike any human speech.\n\nThe letters serve as a direct challenge: this Quran is built from the same letters you use in your daily speech, so if you doubt its divine origin, produce something like it. What follows after these letters typically glorifies the Quran itself or declares its source, and that pattern continues here. The presence of Ḥâ-Mĩm also connects this surah to a family of seven consecutive chapters, all beginning with these same two letters, known as the Ḥawâmîm. They were revealed in Mecca during a period of intense opposition, and they share common themes: the truth of revelation, the folly of idolatry, the certainty of resurrection, and the consolation of the Prophet.\n\nOpening with Ḥâ-Mĩm reminds the believer that behind the familiar letters lies a divine speech that contains guidance, warning, and mercy, and that the human mind is being drawn into an encounter with the transcendent. The abruptness of the letters, followed by a declaration about the Book, also mirrors how divine revelation broke into human history: sudden, undeniable, and demanding a response. So the very first verse, though consisting of only two letters, is a profound gateway into the message that will unfold, alerting the heart to the weight of what it is about to hear.",
  "2": "Having captured attention with the disconnected letters, the surah immediately clarifies the source of the Quran: its revelation, or tanzîl, is decisively from Allah. The Arabic word tanzîl conveys the idea of a gradual sending down from on high, highlighting that this Book did not originate in the mind of the Prophet but descended from the Lord of the worlds. Allah is named here by two majestic attributes: al-ʿAzîz, the Almighty, and al-Ḥakîm, the All-Wise. The pairing of might and wisdom is crucial because it reassures the listener that the revelation carries both irresistible authority and perfect purpose.\n\nThe Almighty does not send down speech out of need or weakness, and the All-Wise does not reveal guidance that is flawed or futile. For the Meccan idolaters who were being asked to abandon the gods of their forefathers, this verse announces that the call to monotheism is backed by the power and wisdom of the One who controls everything. It also comforts the Prophet, who faced rejection and accusations of fabrication; the Book he recites is not his own, but from the Almighty who will protect it and the All-Wise who knows why it must meet resistance before it triumphs. This opening verse of substance, then, roots the entire surah in the reality of divine authorship, making clear that to reject the Quran is to reject the authority and wisdom of Allah Himself.\n\nIt also subtly prepares the heart for the themes ahead: a powerful and wise God creates with purpose, commands justice, sends messengers, holds people accountable, and is fully able to resurrect the dead. All of these ideas will flow naturally from the foundational claim that the revelation of this Book is from Allah, the Almighty, All-Wise.",
  "3": "The verse begins with a definitive statement that the entire universe—the heavens, the earth, and all that exists between them—was not created in vain or as a passing sport. The words “for a purpose” translate the Arabic bil-ḥaqq, which means with truth, justice, and a definitive reason. Creation is therefore an expression of divine wisdom and not a purposeless accident. Adding “and an appointed term” underscores that this cosmic order has a fixed duration; it will one day come to an end, and with that end comes the fulfilment of every purpose for which it was made.\n\nThis directly refutes both the materialist who thinks life ends with death and the idolater who imagines that the world runs by the whims of false gods. When Allah says “We only created,” it establishes the exclusive creative act of the Almighty, leaving no room for partners. Yet despite this unmistakable truth, the disbelievers are turning away from what they have been warned about. The warning encompasses the consequences of rejecting tawhid, the reality of resurrection, and the certainty of judgement.\n\nTheir turning away is not due to lack of evidence; creation itself is a vast, visible sign pointing to its Creator. Rather, it is a stubborn refusal born of arrogance, attachment to ancestral ways, and immersion in worldly desires. The verse, then, frames the entire human drama: a universe full of purpose, a limited lifespan for reflection and action, and a divine warning that many heedlessly ignore. The believer reads this and is reminded to live intentionally, to see every moment as meaningful, and to stay aligned with the purpose for which the heavens and earth themselves were brought into being.",
  "4": "The Prophet is instructed to pose a direct challenge to the idolaters, forcing them to examine the foundations of their worship. The rhetorical question “Have you considered whatever idols you invoke besides Allah?” demands that they reflect on the beings and objects they call upon in prayer and distress. The challenge then narrows to the ultimate proof of divinity: creation. “Show me what they have created on earth!” is devastating because it highlights the absolute impotence of idols.\n\nThey have not brought forth a single grain of sand, a leaf, or a drop of water. The follow-up question, “Or do they have a share in the creation of the heavens?” pushes further, asking whether these false gods participated in the grand cosmic order. The heavens, in their vastness and precision, are an even more undeniable testimony to Allah’s exclusive creative power. After the argument from creation comes the argument from revelation.\n\nThe Prophet is told to say, “Bring me a scripture revealed before this Quran or a shred of knowledge, if what you say is true.” The idolaters cannot produce any earlier divine book that sanctions their polytheism, nor can they offer any remnant of sound, transmitted knowledge that justifies idol worship. The phrase “a shred of knowledge” translates athâratin min ʿilmin, meaning even a trace of reliable learning. The entire verse dismantles idolatry rationally: if your gods create nothing, own nothing in the heavens, and have never sent a single message commanding their worship, then what basis remains for your devotion?\n\nIt is an invitation to use the intellect that Allah Himself bestowed, and it exposes idolatry as a tradition devoid of any evidence, built only on inherited assumption.",
  "5": "This verse builds upon the challenge of the previous one by painting a stark picture of the spiritual condition of the idolater. The question “And who could be more astray” is a rhetorical device implying that no one is further from the truth than such a person. The objects of their supplication are described as utterly unresponsive: they cannot respond to them until the Day of Judgment. That includes wooden idols, stone statues, angels, prophets, or saints whom they call upon besides Allah.\n\nEven if the supplicated beings are righteous servants of God who will be honoured in the Hereafter, they are completely unable to hear, let alone answer, any prayer in this world. The phrase “and are even unaware of their calls” deepens the tragedy. Those being invoked have no knowledge that anyone is calling upon them. They are absent, lifeless, or occupied with their own devotion to Allah.\n\nThe worshiper cries out in need, in fear, in hope, but the one called upon remains in total ignorance. This exposes the irrationality of idolatry: it attaches the heart to what has no awareness, no power, and no capacity to benefit or harm. The verse also implicitly contrasts these false objects of worship with Allah, who is ever near, fully aware, and always able to respond. A person who pours out his soul before a deaf and ignorant object has lost the very purpose of his existence, while one who turns to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing Creator finds his call heard and answered according to divine wisdom.\n\nThe practical lesson is that human desperation needs a responsive God, and only Allah fulfills that need.",
  "6": "The scene shifts to the Day of Resurrection, when the true nature of idolatrous relationships will be exposed. The verse states that when people are gathered for judgement, the very gods they used to invoke will become their enemies and will disown their worship. This is a profound reversal of the intimate devotion the idolaters showed in the world. The beings they called upon—whether idols, false deities, or even righteous figures who were wrongly deified—will declare themselves innocent of the worship that was directed to them.\n\nMany passages in the Quran describe this disowning: the worshipped will say they never commanded such worship, and the worshipers will be left in shame and regret. The verse reveals that idolatry is not a neutral mistake; it creates a relationship of enmity on the day when every soul desperately needs an intercessor and ally. Those who invested their love, fear, and hope in false gods will find those same gods standing against them as witnesses to their misguidance. This outcome is the logical consequence of the earlier verses: if the idols cannot create, cannot hear, and cannot respond, then in the Hereafter they will certainly not defend.\n\nThe Meccan pagans were warned that their ancestral deities, far from bringing them closer to Allah, would become a source of ruin. The believer, reflecting on this verse, understands the supreme importance of purifying worship for Allah alone, because every attachment that takes the place of Allah in the heart will eventually turn into a source of pain and separation on the Day when all veils are lifted.",
  "7": "The surah now turns to the Meccan response to the Quranic message itself. The phrase “Whenever Our clear revelations are recited to them” indicates persistence: repeatedly, as the Prophet recited the verses, the disbelievers heard the unmistakable signs of divine speech. These are called “clear revelations” because they are lucid, self-evident, and distinguished from human speech. Yet the disbelievers, rather than contemplating their clarity, immediately label them “pure magic.\n\n” The word used for magic, siḥr, implies a spell that captivates and deceives. By calling the Quran magic, they simultaneously acknowledged its extraordinary power over their hearts while denying its divine source. They could not dismiss the Quran as poetry, soothsaying, or mere stories, so they resorted to an accusation that robbed it of any moral authority. The phrase “of the truth when it has come to them” exposes the tragedy: truth itself has arrived, clear and undeniable, but they reject it with a label that requires no thinking.\n\nIt is an emotional defence mechanism to avoid submission. This verse echoes the experience of earlier prophets, whose miracles were called magic by stubborn disbelievers. It also consoles the Prophet that the rejection he faces is not due to any shortcoming in the message, but due to a spiritual disease in the hearers. The verse teaches that the truth can be manifestly clear and yet still be refused by hearts that are unwilling to relinquish pride, desire, or ancestral tradition.\n\nThe response required from a sincere person is humble listening and fair reflection, not a preemptive dismissal.",
  "8": "Another accusation of the disbelievers is that the Prophet himself invented the Quran. The verse begins with “Or do they say,” linking it to the previous slander of magic, showing that the disbelievers fluctuated between contradictory accusations: sometimes magic, sometimes fabrication. The Prophet is instructed to reply with calm and rational argument. He says that if he had indeed fabricated it and falsely attributed it to Allah, then the people could offer him no protection from divine punishment.\n\nThis shifts the focus from human debate to divine accountability. The Prophet is not seeking their approval; he fears Allah, who knows the truth of the matter fully. The phrase “He knows best what slurs you indulge about it” acknowledges that the disbelievers are engaging in sustained verbal attacks, but that Allah is aware of every word. “Sufficient is He as a Witness between you and me” means that the ultimate judgement is in Allah’s hands; He will decide who is truthful and who is lying.\n\nThe verse closes with two beautiful divine names: the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful. This is a subtle invitation even for those who slander the Quran. If they would turn from these accusations, repent sincerely, and embrace the truth, they would find Allah’s forgiveness and mercy wide open. So the response to the charge of fabrication is neither anger nor despair; it is a dignified entrustment of the matter to Allah, combined with a window of hope for the accusers.\n\nIt also underscores that the Prophet’s mission is not a personal project; he stands under the authority of the One who sent him, and his sole concern is fulfilling the trust.",
  "9": "The Prophet is commanded to further clarify his human nature and the limits of his knowledge, cutting off any exaggerated claims that might later be made about him. Saying “I am not the first messenger ever sent” means his coming is part of a long chain of prophethood; he is not a novelty, and his message is the same core truth brought by Noah, Abraham, Moses, and Jesus. This also implies that the rejection he faces is not unique; previous messengers were similarly accused and denied. The statement “nor do I know what will happen to me or you” is a profound admission of human limitation.\n\nThe Prophet does not possess independent knowledge of the unseen, of future events, or of his own ultimate fate except what Allah reveals. This reinforces absolute reliance on Allah and shatters any notion that the Prophet is a fortune-teller or a divine being. “I only follow what is revealed to me” defines his mission precisely: he is a follower, not an innovator. The revelation guides his actions, his decisions, and his teachings.\n\nThe conclusion, “And I am only sent with a clear warning,” sums up his role: a warner whose message is unambiguous, leaving no one in ignorance about the consequences of disbelief and disobedience. The verse instills humility in the believer regarding the Prophet: he is honoured as the final messenger, but he is a servant who follows revelation, not an independent source of knowledge. It also invites the disbelievers to assess the message on its own merit rather than demanding from the Prophet what only Allah can provide.",
  "10": "The Prophet is told to present a hypothetical but weighty scenario to the Meccan pagans. The verse asks them to reflect on the possibility that the Quran genuinely originates from Allah while they obstinately deny it. The situation is made more serious by mentioning “a witness from the Children of Israel” who attests to the truth of the Quran and believes. This witness is understood by many commentators to be someone knowledgeable in the previous scriptures who recognises the signs of true prophethood described in the Torah and the Gospel and then accepts Islam.\n\nThe Meccans respected the People of the Book and sometimes consulted them on religious matters, so the testimony of such a witness should have carried weight. Yet their arrogance prevents them from even considering the evidence. The contrast is sharp: a learned person from a scriptural tradition embraces the truth, while the Quraysh, who prided themselves on their nobility, reject it out of pride. The phrase “whereas you act arrogantly” pinpoints the root disease: not lack of proof, but arrogance.\n\nThe verse concludes with a statement of divine principle: “Surely Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people.” Those who persist in wrongdoing, including the arrogance that rejects truth, cut themselves off from divine guidance. This is not because Allah is unwilling to guide, but because their moral posture makes them unreceptive to the light. The lesson is that humility and openness are prerequisites for guidance, and that even the most unlikely people can become believers when their hearts are sincere.",
  "11": "This verse captures the social arrogance of the Quraysh leaders when they saw the weak and the poor among the early Muslims accepting Islam. They looked at the likes of Bilâl, ʿAmmâr, and Suhayb and said, “Had it been something good, they would not have beaten us to it.” In their minds, truth should first be recognised by the elite, the wealthy, and the powerful; that the downtrodden embraced the message was, to them, proof of its falsehood. This is a classic case of judging truth by the social status of its followers rather than by its intrinsic merit.\n\nThe Quran exposes this mindset and then describes their next logical step: since they have already rejected its guidance based on pride, they now need to justify their rejection. So they follow up with a dismissal, calling it “an ancient fabrication.” This is contradictory: earlier they claimed the weak beat them to it, suggesting it was a new movement, but now they label it ancient and therefore irrelevant. The inconsistency shows that the rejection is not based on reasoned evaluation but on a stubborn desire to find any label that wards off the call.\n\nThe verse serves as a warning against the elitism that blinds people to truth. It also comforts the believers that their social standing in the world does not determine their worth before Allah; sincerity, not status, is the criterion.",
  "12": "The surah now connects the Quran to the earlier revelation given to Moses, the Torah. By mentioning that the Book of Moses was a guide and mercy, the verse establishes a continuity of divine guidance. The Torah was a light for the Children of Israel, and now the Quran comes as a confirmation of the truths that were revealed before. The phrase “in the Arabic tongue” highlights that this final scripture is in a clear, accessible language for its primary audience, the Arabs.\n\nThis confirms that divine mercy is not confined to one nation; it flows in the language people understand. The verse then spells out two functions of the Quran: to warn those who do wrong, and to give good news to those who do good. The warning is directed at the wrongdoers, the idolaters, and the arrogant who persist in sin, while the glad tidings are for the muhsinîn, those who excel in faith and righteous deeds. This dual function permeates the entire Quran, balancing fear and hope.\n\nThe Meccans, who were familiar with the concept of the Torah and respected Moses, are being told that what the Prophet brings is not a strange new doctrine but a reaffirmation and completion of the same prophetic tradition. Thus rejecting the Quran is rejecting a message that is essentially the same as the one they claimed to respect in the Torah. The verse reassures the believers that they are part of a long history of guided communities, and it invites the disbelievers to reconsider their stance in light of the clear, Arabic confirmation they now hold.",
  "13": "After describing the rejection of the disbelievers, the surah turns to a beautiful portrait of the faithful. The verse defines true believers as those who declare, with full conviction, that their Lord is Allah, and then istaqâmû—they remain steadfast. This single word, istaqâmû, carries immense weight. It means they hold firmly to that declaration of monotheism, letting it shape their beliefs, character, actions, and entire life direction.\n\nThey do not waver with every trial; they do not mix pure tawhid with hidden shirk; they persist in obedience through ease and hardship. The reward for such steadfastness is described in deeply comforting terms: “there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.” Fear relates to what lies ahead, and grief relates to what has passed. So they are safe from terror in the future—the horrors of the grave, the Day of Judgement, and the Fire—and they will not sorrow over missed opportunities or past losses.\n\nThis promise echoes the Quranic description of the allies of Allah. The verse is a powerful reminder that the path of faith is not merely a momentary emotion or a verbal utterance; it is a lifelong commitment to stability upon the truth. The absence of fear and grief is the ultimate peace that every human soul seeks, and it is found only in the sincere and sustained recognition that Allah is the Lord.",
  "14": "The promise of the previous verse is now given a concrete, eternal home. Those who combine the testimony of tawhid with steadfastness are described as “the residents of Paradise.” The word used implies companionship and permanence; they will be the people of the Garden, belonging to it as their true and everlasting abode. The phrase “staying there forever” removes any fear of termination, decay, or eviction.\n\nParadise is not a temporary resort but an eternal realm of delight. The verse explicitly states that this is “a reward for what they used to do,” which clarifies that while salvation is ultimately by Allah’s mercy, deeds are the tangible expression of faith and the basis for degrees of reward. The Quran consistently ties eternal outcomes to earthly actions, not to empty claims. This linkage is an encouragement to the believer to view every act of goodness, every restraint from sin, and every moment of patience as an investment that will bear everlasting fruit.\n\nIt also counters the fatalistic idea that deeds are irrelevant. The verse completes the portrait of the blessed: they trust in Allah, they live uprightly, and their final destination is a paradise of unimaginable bliss that they will never have to leave. For the early Muslims facing persecution in Mecca, these verses were a source of immense hope, a reminder that the hardship of the moment was nothing compared to the eternal reward.",
  "15": "The surah now addresses the relationship between children and parents, a theme deeply connected to the gratitude owed to the Creator. Allah commands humanity to treat parents with ihsân—the most excellent conduct. The verse immediately provides the reason by describing the mother’s unique suffering: she bore the child in hardship upon hardship, and then delivered the child in hardship. The accumulation of weakness, pain, and exhaustion is a powerful motivator for gratitude.\n\nThe period of bearing and weaning is specified as thirty months. Scholars have deduced from this, along with another verse that prescribes two full years for weaning, that the minimum period of gestation is six months. The verse then transitions to a beautiful picture of a righteous person who, upon reaching full maturity at the age of forty, makes a heartfelt supplication. Forty represents the completion of physical and intellectual strength, a time when life’s experiences should have deepened wisdom.\n\nSuch a person prays for inspiration to be truly thankful for the favours bestowed upon him and his parents. The prayer includes a request to perform righteous deeds that please Allah, showing that gratitude is not just a feeling but translates into action. The supplicant also asks Allah to instil righteousness in his offspring, demonstrating concern for the spiritual legacy that continues beyond one’s own life. The prayer ends with a declaration of repentance and submission to Allah.\n\nThe entire verse thus draws a complete circle: the child who was once carried in weakness grows to recognise the favours of his Lord and his parents, and channels that recognition into a life of devotion, goodness, and concern for the next generation.",
  "16": "Allah now declares the blessed outcome for those described in the previous verse, the righteous children who honour their parents, reach mature reflection, and dedicate themselves to supplication and good deeds. The verse states that it is from these people that Allah will accept the best of their actions. The phrase “accept the good they did” translates a concept of graceful reception; Allah does not merely note their deeds, He accepts them with pleasure and multiplies their reward. Alongside this acceptance, Allah will overlook their misdeeds.\n\nThis overlooking means that the sins and shortcomings that inevitably cling to every human being will be passed over, forgiven, and not held against them. The verse then places these people in the company of the residents of Paradise, indicating that they are counted among the righteous inhabitants of the Garden. The promise they were given is described as “the true promise,” the waʿd al-ṣidq, which is Allah’s unfailing pledge of eternal reward for the believers. The connection to the previous verse is seamless: the one who honours parents, reflects on divine favours, prays for gratitude and righteous deeds, and cares for the faith of his children, will find his record purified and his place in Paradise assured.\n\nThis is a message of immense hope for every believer who strives to balance duties to Allah and duties to parents. It also shows that family relationships, when built on faith and righteousness, become a means of drawing nearer to Allah and attaining His pleasure in the Hereafter.",
  "17": "In stark contrast to the dutiful child, this verse presents the rebellious one who scolds his believing parents when they remind him of resurrection and accountability. His dismissive words, “Enough with you!”, show irritation and disrespect. He argues that the idea of being brought forth from the grave is absurd because countless earlier generations have perished and never returned. This is a materialist objection rooted in seeing only the physical world and forgetting the power of the Creator.\n\nThe parents, who are described as crying to Allah for help, are heartbroken. Their plea, “Pity you. Have faith! Surely Allah’s promise is true,” reveals their deep love and desperation.\n\nThey use the gentlest expression, waylaka, which means ‘woe to you’ or ‘pity you,’ not a harsh curse, but a sorrowful expression of concern. They appeal to the truth of Allah’s promise, but the denier stubbornly insists that such talk is “nothing but ancient fables.” This replicates the exact attitude of the Quraysh disbelievers towards the Quran itself. The verse thus illustrates a tragic family dynamic: the generational divide is not merely about age but about faith versus disbelief.\n\nThe child’s arrogance has closed his heart to the most sincere advice. This scene serves as a warning that one’s own family may become a test, and that true loyalty is to the truth, even when it pains those we love.",
  "18": "The verse identifies the arrogant deniers described in the previous verse as those upon whom the word of punishment has become binding, just as it did for earlier nations of jinn and humankind who rejected their messengers. The phrase “the fate” translates al-qawl, the decree or word of punishment that Allah pronounces against persistent rejecters. This decree is not arbitrary; it is “justified” because they followed in the footsteps of the ruined nations, sharing their rebellion, arrogance, and mockery of divine warning. The mention of both jinn and humans broadens the scope, indicating that accountability and the consequences of disbelief transcend the human realm.\n\nThe jinn, like humans, have received guidance and will be judged. The final clause, “for they were truly losers,” gives the reason for their ruin. They are losers in the most absolute sense: they lost their souls, their opportunities, their eternal happiness, and exchanged the truth for falsehood. This description is a sobering reminder that no one is immune to the patterns that destroyed earlier peoples.\n\nIf the same attitudes of denial, mockery, and disrespect for divine reminders are present, the same outcome will follow. The verse also reinforces the Quranic theme that history is not a random sequence of events; it contains lessons for those who reflect. The fate of past nations is not merely a story but a warning that the sunan of Allah, His established ways, do not change.",
  "19": "After presenting the contrasting portraits of the righteous and the rebellious, the surah now declares the universal principle of divine justice that will be applied on the Day of Judgement. The two groups—the believers and the disbelievers—will be classified into degrees, or ranks, based entirely on their deeds. The phrase “according to what they have done” underscores that eternal standing is not determined by lineage, wealth, or worldly status, but solely by the quality and sincerity of one’s actions. The purpose of this ranking is “so He may fully reward all,” meaning that every soul will receive exactly what its deeds have earned, down to the smallest atom of good or evil.\n\nThe statement “and none will be wronged” is a guarantee of perfect justice. No one’s good deed will be diminished, and no one’s evil will be exaggerated. This verse provides a balanced perspective that encourages both hope and fear. The believer strives for excellence, knowing that every effort is recorded and will contribute to his rank in Paradise.\n\nThe wrongdoer is warned that his deeds are also being recorded and will determine his level of recompense. The concept of ranks implies that even within Paradise there are levels, and within the Fire there are levels, all assigned with flawless equity. This principle frees the heart from envy and competition over worldly distinctions; what truly matters is the rank one achieves in the sight of Allah through righteous conduct.",
  "20": "The scene shifts dramatically to the moment when the disbelievers are confronted with the Fire. The word “exposed” translates yuʿraḍûn, conveying a sense of being brought near and made to face the horrifying reality they denied. The address they receive is piercing: they are told that they already exhausted their share of good and pleasant things during their earthly life, and they fully indulged in them. This does not mean that enjoyment itself is condemned; rather, they made worldly pleasure their ultimate goal, forgetting the Hereafter entirely.\n\nThey consumed their portion of lawful and unlawful delights without any concern for gratitude, accountability, or the rights of others. The consequence is that today they receive the torment of disgrace as a fitting recompense. Two specific sins are highlighted: arrogance throughout the land without any right, and rebelliousness. Arrogance here means treating themselves as superior, rejecting the truth, and oppressing others.\n\n“Without any right” emphasises that there is no legitimate basis for such haughtiness. Rebelliousness, or fusûq, refers to persistent departure from obedience to Allah. The torment is called “the torment of disgrace” because it is accompanied by humiliation; just as they sought honour through wrongful pride, they are now covered in shame. This verse serves as a powerful warning that this life is a season of sowing, not just reaping.\n\nThose who take their fill now and ignore the coming harvest will find themselves empty-handed and disgraced when it matters most.",
  "21": "The surah now transitions from the general warning to a specific historical example that the Meccans knew well: the story of the people of ʿȂd and their prophet Hûd. Hûd is tenderly described as “the brother of ʿȂd,” not a stranger or an outsider, but one of their own kin, whose sincerity and concern for them could not be questioned. He was sent to a people who inhabited the sand-hills, al-Aḥqâf, a region of towering dunes in the southern part of the Arabian Peninsula. This geographical detail grounds the story in a real place, reminding the Quraysh that the ruins they passed on their trade journeys were not legends but remnants of a destroyed civilisation.\n\nThe verse adds an important parenthetical remark: “there were certainly warners before and after him.” This situates Hûd within a long chain of prophets. He was not the first to deliver the message of tawhid, nor the last, showing that Allah’s guidance has been a continuous mercy to humanity. Hûd’s message is the unchanging core of all prophetic calls: “Worship none but Allah.\n\n” This is the simple and profound declaration that shattered the idolatrous traditions of his people. Then comes the warning: “I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous day.” Hûd’s fear is not a personal anxiety but a compassionate dread that his people are heading towards a catastrophic punishment if they persist in their denial. The “tremendous day” could refer to the Day of Judgement or a severe worldly punishment, or both, as was the case with ʿȂd who faced a devastating wind in this life, followed by the reckoning in the Hereafter.\n\nThe Meccans, hearing this, were meant to see themselves mirrored in the people of ʿȂd: prosperous, powerful, and yet rejecting the call of a sincere brother from among them.",
  "22": "The response of ʿȂd to Hûd mirrors the arrogance of the Quraysh to Prophet Muḥammad. Instead of contemplating the message of pure monotheism, they accused Hûd of having a hidden agenda: “Have you come to turn us away from our gods?” Their attachment to their false deities was so deep that any call to abandon them was perceived as a hostile takeover of their identity and heritage. They could not imagine that someone might genuinely care for their spiritual wellbeing; they assumed a motive of power or manipulation.\n\nThen, in a display of defiant pride, they challenged Hûd to bring on the punishment he threatened. The phrase “Bring us then whatever you threaten us with, if what you say is true” is not a sincere request for proof, but a sarcastic and arrogant dare. They believed that their strength, their impressive buildings, and their advanced civilisation made them immune to any divine retribution. This attitude reveals a fatal misunderstanding of the nature of divine punishment: it is not something humans can summon on demand, nor is it a sign of weakness if it is delayed.\n\nThe demand to hasten the punishment is a recurring theme with many nations, showing that when people feel secure in their worldly power, they mock warnings and treat the Hereafter as a distant myth. The verse captures the tragic collision between a sincere warner who fears for his people and a people who mistake his compassion for a threat to their comfortable traditions. The same dynamic was unfolding in Mecca, where the leaders dared the Prophet to bring down the stones from the sky if he was truthful.",
  "23": "Hûd’s reply is a model of prophetic humility, clarity, and firmness. He does not claim to control the punishment or know when it will descend. He states, “The knowledge of its time is only with Allah.” This is a crucial corrective: the timing and nature of divine punishment belong exclusively to Allah’s wisdom.\n\nA messenger is not a distributor of vengeance; he is a deliverer of a message. Hûd then defines his mission precisely: “I only convey to you what I have been sent with.” He has no personal agenda, no ability to alter the message, and no power to bring about the consequences on his own. His duty is delivery, and whether they believe or not, he has fulfilled his responsibility.\n\nHis final statement, “But I can see that you are a people acting ignorantly,” is not an insult but a diagnosis. Their ignorance is not a lack of intelligence; ʿȂd were materially advanced and skilled. Rather, it is jahl in its Quranic sense: an impulsive, arrogant, and irrational behaviour that springs from passions and pride rather than knowledge and reflection. By demanding punishment and mocking the warner, they were acting against their own best interests, like a patient who abuses the doctor bringing the cure.\n\nThis verse teaches that the role of religious guides is to convey the truth with clarity, not to force results or to know the unseen. It also warns that demanding immediate tangible proof while ignoring the clear signs already given is a sign of deep ignorance.",
  "24": "The scene of the punishment’s arrival is described with dramatic irony. After a period of drought, the people of ʿȂd saw a dense cloud advancing towards their valleys. Their immediate reaction was one of joy and relief: they said happily, “This is a cloud bringing us rain.” Their minds were fixed on their immediate worldly needs, and they interpreted the natural phenomenon through their hopes and desires.\n\nThey had no inkling that this cloud was not a bearer of mercy but a vessel of destruction. Then Hûd’s voice cuts through their celebration with a terrifying clarification: “No, it is what you sought to hasten: a fierce wind carrying a painful punishment!” The very thing they had mockingly dared him to bring had now materialised, but in a form they did not expect. The cloud was not carrying water droplets but a screaming, violent wind that would uproot everything.\n\nThis sudden reversal illustrates how divine punishment can come from directions people least anticipate, and how what appears to be a blessing can, for the guilty, be the vehicle of wrath. The verse also underscores the folly of demanding punishment as a proof; when it comes, it leaves no room for repentance or escape. The irony is painful: they were rejoicing at the sight of their own doom. For the Quraysh, this was a vivid warning that their mockery of the Prophet’s warnings could end in a similarly unexpected catastrophe.\n\nThe believer is reminded to never feel secure from Allah’s plan and to receive His signs with humility rather than presumptuous interpretations.",
  "25": "The wind, acting by the command of its Lord, became an instrument of total annihilation. The verse says “It destroyed everything,” meaning every structure, every possession, and every living being in its path. The phrase “by the command of its Lord” is crucial: the wind did not act as a random natural disaster; it was a soldier from the soldiers of Allah, obedient to His decree. The destruction was so thorough that nothing remained visible except their ruins, their abandoned dwellings that stood as silent testimony to their former strength.\n\nThe ruins of ʿȂd’s stone-built homes in the region of al-Aḥqâf were known to the Arabs, serving as a perpetual reminder of what happens to those who defy Allah’s messengers. The concluding sentence, “This is how We reward the wicked people,” establishes a universal principle. It is not merely a historical report; it is an ongoing divine law that wickedness and persistent rejection of truth lead to ruin in both this world and the next. This verse serves as a sobering reflection that human strength, no matter how impressive, is nothing before the command of Allah.\n\nThe wind, which usually brings life through pollination and rain, becomes a destroyer when carrying a divine decree. The same element that gives life can take it, reminding humanity that everything in the universe is a servant of its Creator and will act as He wills.",
  "26": "The address shifts directly to the Meccan pagans, drawing an explicit comparison between them and the destroyed people of ʿȂd. Allah tells the Quraysh that He had established the ʿȂd with a power and prosperity that far exceeded what the Meccans enjoyed. ʿȂd had greater physical strength, more advanced architecture, wider dominion, and longer lifespans. Despite this, they were destroyed. If a mightier nation could not escape punishment, how could the less powerful Quraysh expect immunity?\n\nThe verse then lists the fundamental faculties that should have guided ʿȂd: hearing, sight, and intellect. They could hear the warnings of Hûd, they could see the signs of creation around them, and they had intellects to process and understand the truth. Yet these faculties were of no benefit whatsoever. The reason is given: they persisted in denying Allah’s signs.\n\nThe faculties were sound, but the will was corrupted by arrogance and attachment to false tradition. The outcome was that they were overwhelmed—literally surrounded and destroyed—by the very thing they used to ridicule. They had mocked Hûd’s warnings of a painful torment, and that torment descended upon them exactly as described. The same pattern applied to the Quraysh, who were ridiculing the Prophet’s warnings.\n\nThis verse is a profound lesson about the misuse of God-given faculties. Hearing, sight, and intellect are trusts; when they are employed to reject truth rather than embrace it, they become witnesses against their owners. The tragedy is not in having weak abilities, but in failing to use strong ones for the purpose they were created.",
  "27": "Allah reminds the Quraysh of a fact they could not deny: the societies that surrounded them in the Arabian Peninsula—ʿȂd, Thamûd, the people of Lot, and Madyan—had all been destroyed. The ruins of these nations were visible along their trade routes, and their stories were known in oral tradition. The destruction was not arbitrary; it came after Allah had “varied the signs.” This means that the proofs and warnings were presented in diverse, repeated, and compelling ways.\n\nThe messengers came with clear arguments, miracles, and scripture, all designed to touch the heart and intellect. The purpose of this variety was so perhaps they would return, meaning turn back from their disbelief and sin to the Right Path. The word “perhaps” here does not indicate uncertainty in Allah’s knowledge, but rather expresses the wisdom of leaving the door open for human choice and transformation. The destruction of previous nations was a manifestation of both justice and mercy: justice for the persistent rejecters, and a merciful warning to subsequent generations like the Quraysh.\n\nThe Meccans were intended to learn from these nearby examples, to see that the very roads they travelled were built over the graves of arrogant empires, and to realise that the same sunan of Allah applied to them. The verse invites reflection on history as a teacher; those who fail to learn from the past are doomed to repeat its fatal mistakes.",
  "28": "The surah now returns to the central theme of idolatry, asking a devastating question about the destroyed nations and, by extension, the Meccan idolaters. The false gods they had adopted were not always simple pieces of wood; many were beings like angels, jinn, or righteous humans whom they worshipped hoping they would act as intermediaries to bring them closer to Allah. The phrase “hoping to get closer to Him” exposes the internal justification of idolatry: they knew Allah was the Supreme Creator, but they believed these lesser deities could intercede and draw them near. Yet when divine punishment descended on ʿȂd and others, these supposed intermediaries and gods were nowhere to be found.\n\n“Why then did they not come to their aid?” is the crushing question. They vanished, offered no protection, and could not stop the wind or the earthquake. The verse answers plainly: “Instead, they failed them.” They were utterly lost and absent, leaving their worshipers to face destruction alone.\n\nThe concluding sentence explains the root cause: “That is the result of their lies and their fabrications.” The entire system of polytheism was built on falsehood, on names and concepts they invented without any sanction from Allah. On the day of reckoning, falsehood collapses, and only truth remains. For the Quraysh, who also claimed that their idols were intercessors with Allah, this verse was a direct and urgent warning that their reliance on such fabricated deities would end in the same catastrophic disappointment.",
  "29": "After the long segment about human rejection, the surah offers a striking contrast in the receptivity of a group of jinn to the Quran. The Prophet is told to remember the occasion when Allah directed a company of jinn towards him during his recitation. According to authentic reports, this occurred when the Prophet was praying or reciting Quran in a valley, and a group of passing jinn stopped to listen. The jinn are a creation made of smokeless fire, accountable to divine commands, and they have believers and disbelievers among them.\n\nUpon hearing the Quran, they immediately recognised its divine origin. Their first reaction was to say to one another, “Listen quietly!” This command shows their profound respect and eagerness to understand. They silenced one another to absorb the recitation without distraction, a model of sincere listening that contrasts sharply with the Quraysh who would make noise to drown out the Quran.\n\nWhen the recitation finished, they did not keep the treasure to themselves; they returned to their community as warners, carrying the message they had just received. They transformed from mere listeners into preachers of truth. This brief narrative carries powerful lessons: the truth can penetrate hearts that human arrogance has closed, the jinn who are unseen showed more humility than the visible human elite, and the Quran is a guidance for all sentient beings. It also comforted the Prophet, showing him that while his own people rejected him, other creatures of Allah were embracing the message in the unseen.",
  "30": "The content of the jinn’s preaching is recorded in this and the following verses, giving us a rare insight into their call. They addressed their community with a warm and inclusive “O our fellow jinn,” establishing their shared identity and concern. Their testimony begins with a remarkable statement: they heard a scripture revealed after Moses. This indicates that the jinn had some awareness of earlier revelations, particularly the Torah given to Moses, and they recognised that prophethood had continued.\n\nThe Quran is described as coming “after Moses,” confirming what came before it. This confirmation of earlier scriptures is a major theme of the Quran, which sees itself as the culmination and guardian of the original truths revealed to all prophets. The jinn then declare that this scripture “guides to the truth and the Straight Way.” The truth here means the correct beliefs about Allah, the afterlife, and the purpose of existence.\n\nThe Straight Way, aṣ-ṣirâṭ al-mustaqîm, is the path of conduct that leads directly to Allah’s pleasure and Paradise. Their words are a concise and accurate summary of the Quran’s function, which the Meccan disbelievers were unable to articulate because of their prejudice. The fact that the jinn—who have no tribal loyalties to the Prophet, no ancestral pride in Arab paganism—could so readily and clearly articulate the message is a devastating indictment of the human disbelievers who had the same Quran recited to them daily yet persisted in calling it magic or ancient fables.",
  "31": "The jinn continue their passionate call to their people, urging an immediate and positive response. “Respond to the caller of Allah” identifies the Prophet Muḥammad as Allah’s summoner, the one who invites all creatures to their Lord. The command to “respond” implies not just passive listening but active obedience and acceptance. Following this, they say “and believe in him,” meaning to affirm the message the caller brings and to trust in his truthfulness.\n\nThe jinn then outline the magnificent reward for responding: forgiveness of sins and protection from a painful punishment. Forgiveness deals with the past, wiping clean the record of transgressions, while protection from painful punishment deals with the future, guaranteeing safety from Hellfire. This is the core promise of the Quranic message, and the jinn present it with beautiful simplicity, without arguing, debating, or making excuses. Their call is an invitation of mercy, not a threat.\n\nThe fact that jinn, who are generally more hidden from human interaction, would so clearly articulate the gospel of hope exposes the stubbornness of the human disbelievers who had the same invitation and rejected it. This verse also emphasises the universality of the Prophet’s mission: he is not merely a messenger to the Arabs or to humans, but to all creatures of Allah who are subject to moral responsibility.",
  "32": "The jinn balance their glad tidings with a clear warning, demonstrating that true preaching must contain both hope and fear. They declare that whoever fails to respond to Allah’s caller will find himself absolutely helpless. The phrase “will have no escape on earth” means that no matter where such a person flees, he cannot elude Allah’s grasp. The earth, vast as it is, provides no refuge from divine accountability.\n\nThe addition “nor will they have any protectors against Him” dismantles the entire basis of idolatry, where people took gods as intercessors and protectors from harm. On the Day of Judgement, there will be no allies, no lawyers, no bribes, and no false gods to shield a person from the consequences of his rejection. The jinn conclude with a definitive judgement: “It is they who are clearly astray.” They are not merely slightly mistaken; they are in manifest, self-evident misguidance because they have turned away from the one true source of guidance and protection.\n\nThis warning from the jinn would have been deeply unsettling for the Quraysh to hear. A hidden nation of unseen beings has understood the stakes of the Prophet’s message with more clarity than the human elite of Mecca. The verse reinforces the Quranic message that rejection of the call is not a trivial choice; it is a catastrophic error with eternal consequences, leaving a soul utterly alone and exposed in the face of the Almighty.",
  "33": "The surah returns to the central Meccan objection: the denial of the resurrection. The disbelievers found it inconceivable that decayed bones and scattered dust could be reanimated. This verse presents a rational argument from the greater to the smaller. It begins with “Do they not realize,” appealing to the intellect that observes the heavens and the earth.\n\nThe creation of the entire cosmos, with its vast galaxies, intricate laws, and boundless space, is undeniably a greater feat than reassembling human bodies from the earth. The phrase “and did not tire in creating them” is a powerful refutation of any notion that the act of creation exhausted or diminished Allah. The idea that a god could become weary is found in some ancient myths, but the Quran categorically rejects it. Allah’s power is infinite and His act of creation was effortless.\n\nIf He created all of this without fatigue, then bringing the dead back to life is, for Him, an utterly trivial matter. The verse answers its own question with an emphatic “Yes indeed!”, and then declares “He is certainly Most Capable of everything.” The Arabic word qadîr implies not just ability but boundless, perpetual power that can accomplish anything at any moment. The argument is so clear that only stubbornness can reject it.\n\nFor the believer, contemplating the heavens and earth becomes a daily reminder that the same Lord who hung the stars and spread the earth will surely resurrect every soul for account.",
  "34": "The surah now portrays the confirmation of resurrection in the Hereafter itself. The disbelievers who spent their lives mocking the concept of a Day of Judgement will be exposed to the Fire, brought face to face with the very reality they denied. They will be asked a question that is not for information but for acknowledgement and rebuke: “Is this not the truth?” The truth they ridiculed, the truth they called ancient fables, is now an inescapable, tangible reality before their eyes.\n\nTheir answer will be immediate and emphatic: “Absolutely, by our Lord!” The oath “by our Lord” is significant because in the world they associated partners with Allah, but now all false gods have vanished, and they address Allah directly as their sole Lord. Their acknowledgment, however, comes too late. The final pronouncement is chilling: “Then taste the punishment for your disbelief.\n\n” The word “taste” implies a direct, personal, and sensory experience of the torment. It is linked specifically to their disbelief—not just any sin, but the foundational rejection of Allah’s signs and the denial of the meeting with Him. This verse corresponds to the earlier scene in verse 20 where the disbelievers are told they exhausted their pleasures. Here, the bitter truth is swallowed instead.\n\nThe dialogue form makes the scene vivid and terrifying, a drama where the denier is forced to testify against himself. It serves as an urgent warning that the time for “intellectual objections” to the Hereafter will expire, and what will remain is only the unalterable consequence of one’s chosen path.",
  "35": "The surah concludes with a powerful exhortation to the Prophet and, by extension, to every believer who carries the message of truth. The command “So endure patiently” comes as a direct consequence of all that has been narrated: the stories of earlier prophets, the rejection of the Quraysh, the arrogance of the powerful, and the certainty of divine judgment. The Prophet is told to model his patience after ulû al-ʿazm, the Messengers of Firm Resolve. These are understood to be a select group of prophets—commonly identified as Noah, Abraham, Moses, Jesus, and Muḥammad—who faced the most intense trials with unshakeable constancy.\n\nPatience is not passive; it is a firm, active perseverance in the face of opposition, mockery, and hardship. The verse then instructs the Prophet not to seek to hasten the torment for the deniers. It is natural for a human being who is mocked and harmed to wish for a swift punishment upon his oppressors, but the divine timing is based on wisdom and mercy, not human emotion. The day of reckoning will come, and when it does, the disbelievers will perceive their entire worldly life as having lasted no more than an hour of a single day.\n\nThis metaphor illustrates the fleeting nature of earthly existence and the suddenness with which the Hereafter will dawn. The statement “This is a sufficient warning!” affirms that the Quran contains everything necessary for guidance and warning; no further signs are needed. The surah ends with a rhetorical question that summarises its entire message: “Then, will anyone be destroyed except the rebellious people?” Destruction is not random; it is the earned outcome of rebellion against Allah.\n\nThose who break away from obedience, who persist in transgression and arrogance, are the ones who will be utterly ruined. This closing verse balances divine justice with divine wisdom, offering a final call to reflection and a reassurance to the patient believer that the outcome is certain and the victory belongs to those who remain steadfast."
};
